@startuml Parallel Pipeline Module Dependencies Diagram

/'
    Parallel Pipeline Module Dependencies Diagram
    ===========================================

    This diagram shows the dependency relationships between modules in the
    decoupled parallel pipeline architecture. It illustrates:

    - Layered architecture (Application → Core → Extension → Integration)
    - Dependency directions and coupling levels
    - Optional vs required dependencies
    - External system integrations

    Dependency Types:
    - Solid arrows: Required dependencies (compile-time)
    - Dashed arrows: Optional dependencies (runtime)
    - Colored packages: Different architectural layers

    Generated: 2026-01-05
    Version: 1.0
'/

title Parallel Pipeline Module Dependencies Diagram

' Application Layer (Blue)
package "Application Layer" as AppLayer #LightBlue {
    package "parallel_pipeline.h" as MainHeader {
        [parallel_pipeline.h]
        note right: Unified API entry point\nBackward compatible\nIncludes all sub-modules
    }
}

' Core Layer (Green)
package "Core Layer" as CoreLayer #LightGreen {
    package "parallel_task.h" as TaskHeader {
        [parallel_task.h]
        note right: Task lifecycle management\nState transitions\nThreading
    }

    package "parallel_workflow.h" as WorkflowHeader {
        [parallel_workflow.h]
        note right: Workflow orchestration\nDependency resolution\nConcurrency control
    }
}

' Extension Layer (Yellow)
package "Extension Layer" as ExtLayer #LightYellow {
    package "parallel_dataflow.h" as DataFlowHeader {
        [parallel_dataflow.h]
        note right: Data stream integration\nHybrid task support\nStream registration
    }

    package "data_stream_service.h" as StreamServiceHeader {
        [data_stream_service.h]
        note right: Stream lifecycle\nData buffering\nSynchronization
    }

    package "workflow_monitor.h" as MonitorHeader {
        [workflow_monitor.h]
        note right: Metrics collection\nPerformance monitoring\nHealth checks
    }
}

' Integration Layer (Pink)
package "Integration Layer" as IntLayer #LightPink {
    package "parallel_adapters.h" as AdaptersHeader {
        [parallel_adapters.h]
        note right: External system adapters\nProtocol abstractions\nPluggable interfaces
    }
}

' External Dependencies (Gray)
package "External Dependencies" as ExtDeps #LightGray {
    package "System Headers" as SysHeaders {
        [mycommon.h]
        [mytrace.h]
        [fsm_os_adapter.h]
        [task_pipeline.h]
        [parallel_fsm.h]
    }

    package "Data Stream" as DataStream {
        [data_stream.h]
        note right: Low-level data streaming\nBuffer management\nThread safety
    }

    package "External Systems" as ExtSystems {
        [MQ Libraries]
        [Database Drivers]
        [REST Libraries]
        [File Systems]
        note right: Third-party libraries\nNetwork protocols\nStorage systems
    }
}

' Main header includes all modules
MainHeader --> TaskHeader : includes
MainHeader --> WorkflowHeader : includes
MainHeader --> DataFlowHeader : includes
MainHeader --> StreamServiceHeader : includes
MainHeader --> MonitorHeader : includes
MainHeader --> AdaptersHeader : includes

' Core layer dependencies
WorkflowHeader --> TaskHeader : requires

' Extension layer dependencies
DataFlowHeader --> TaskHeader : extends
DataFlowHeader --> StreamServiceHeader : uses
DataFlowHeader --> DataStream : depends

' Workflow optional extensions
WorkflowHeader -.-> DataFlowHeader : optional
WorkflowHeader -.-> MonitorHeader : optional
WorkflowHeader -.-> AdaptersHeader : optional

' Adapter framework dependencies
AdaptersHeader -.-> ExtSystems : integrates

' External dependencies
TaskHeader --> SysHeaders : depends
WorkflowHeader --> SysHeaders : depends
StreamServiceHeader --> DataStream : depends

' Coupling levels visualization
note as CouplingNote
    **Dependency Coupling Levels:**

    **Tight Coupling (Required):**
    - Core modules depend on system headers
    - Workflow depends on task management
    - Data flow extends task functionality

    **Loose Coupling (Optional):**
    - Workflow can use monitoring/dataflow/adapters optionally
    - Adapters integrate with external systems via interfaces
    - Extensions can be compiled out for minimal builds

    **Zero Coupling (Independent):**
    - Adapter implementations are pluggable
    - Monitoring can be disabled at runtime
    - Data streams can be used independently
end note

' Architecture benefits
note as BenefitsNote
    **Architecture Benefits:**

    **Maintainability:**
    - Single Responsibility per module
    - Clear dependency boundaries
    - Isolated testing possible

    **Extensibility:**
    - New adapters without touching core
    - Additional monitoring without workflow changes
    - New data stream types independently

    **Flexibility:**
    - Optional features can be disabled
    - Different adapter combinations
    - Runtime configuration

    **Compatibility:**
    - Existing code unchanged
    - Gradual migration path
    - Backward compatibility maintained
end note

' Usage scenarios
note as UsageScenarios
    **Usage Scenarios:**

    **Minimal Build:**
    parallel_pipeline.h → parallel_task.h + parallel_workflow.h

    **Full Featured:**
    parallel_pipeline.h → all modules

    **Data Processing:**
    parallel_pipeline.h → parallel_dataflow.h + data_stream_service.h

    **System Integration:**
    parallel_pipeline.h → parallel_adapters.h + external libraries

    **Monitoring:**
    parallel_pipeline.h → workflow_monitor.h
end note

@enduml
